<!--
  Purple Team Home Lab — Wazuh custom rules (v3)

  ===========================================================================
  1) LSASS access (100200 / 100201 / 100205)
  ===========================================================================

  WHY THE FIRST ATTEMPT PRODUCED NOTHING
  ......................................
  A custom LSASS rule was deployed during v3 and never alerted once — 0 hits
  across every archived alert file. The first reading was "no attack has been
  run since it was added", which was true and therefore hid the real cause.
  The rule bound to:

      <if_group>sysmon_event10</if_group>

  The real group name is `sysmon_event_10` — with an underscore before the
  number. One missing character, and the rule was loaded, counted as coverage,
  and silently discarded. Wazuh had been saying so on every start:

      wazuh-analysisd: WARNING: (7610): Group 'sysmon_event10' was not
      found. Invalid 'if_group'. Rule '100200' will be ignored.

  That line was available from `wazuh-analysisd -t` at any point. The lesson is
  not the typo; it is that a silent rule was trusted as coverage without ever
  asking the engine whether it had loaded.

  Two group names are easy to confuse, and only one works as a parent:
    * `sysmon_event_10` — assigned to EVERY Sysmon EID 10 event by the
      eventchannel decoder. This is the correct parent for a new detection.
    * `sysmon_eid10_detections` — the enclosing group of 0945-sysmon_id_10.xml,
      carried only by events that already matched one of its rules. Using it as
      a parent would scope a rule to events another rule had already caught.

  WHY THE PARENT IS A GROUP AND NOT <if_sid>92900</if_sid>
  ........................................................
  A second draft of this file bound to <if_sid>92900</if_sid>, reasoning that a
  concrete SID is more stable than a group name. Testing it proved the draft
  could never fire, for the same structural reason as the original bug.
  Built-in rule 92900 carries its own mask filter:

      <rule id="92900" level="12">
        <if_group>sysmon_event_10</if_group>
        <field name="win.eventdata.targetImage">(?i)lsass\.exe</field>
        <field name="win.eventdata.grantedAccess">(?i)(0x1010|0x40)</field>

  So 92900 only matches 0x1010 and 0x40. A child of 92900 that requires a
  full-access or MiniDumpWriteDump mask is mutually exclusive with its own
  parent. Verified by experiment: a PowerShell OpenProcess against lsass.exe
  with PROCESS_ALL_ACCESS (0x1FFFFF) on THEPUNISHER produced no alert at all.

  That experiment also measured a real gap in Wazuh's default ruleset:

      0x1010    Defender's routine read   -> alerts at LEVEL 12 (32 times
                                             in this lab, all 32 benign)
      0x1fffff  full-access handle         -> NO ALERT
      0x1438    MiniDumpWriteDump          -> NO ALERT

  The severity is inverted: the default rule is wrong every time on the benign
  mask and silent on the aggressive ones. 100200 covers the silent half.

  WHY THE ACCESS-MASK LIST IS SHORT
  .................................
  A first draft matched 0x1010 and 0x1410 as well. Measured against 935 real
  EID 10 reads of lsass.exe sampled from THEPUNISHER's own Sysmon log, that
  draft would have alerted 90 times at level 12, of which 89 were ordinary
  platform behaviour:

      79  0x1410    WmiPrvSE.exe
       4  0x1410    vmtoolsd.exe
       3  0x1410    wazuh-agent.exe
       2  0x1fffff  wazuh-agent.exe      <- the EDR agent reading LSASS
       1  0x1410    taskmgr.exe
       1  0x1f3fff  powershell.exe       <- the only one worth triage

  A 98.9% false-positive rate is not a detection, it is an alert generator.
  Restricting to full / MiniDumpWriteDump masks and excluding the platform
  sources measured above brings the same sample to a single alert — the
  PowerShell read at 0x1f3fff. 0x1010 and 0x1410 are left to v4 as a
  low-severity hunt tier; they are legitimate mimikatz masks, but on this
  baseline they are indistinguishable from WMI.

  WHY BACKSLASHES ARE WRITTEN \\+
  ...............................
  Wazuh emits Windows paths in eventdata with DOUBLED backslashes. Checked
  with repr() rather than print(), which cannot show the difference:

      sourceImage repr = 'C:\\\\ProgramData\\\\Microsoft\\\\...\\\\MsMpEng.exe'
                         12 backslashes for 6 path separators

  A pattern anchored on exactly one backslash silently fails to match. `\\+`
  matches either form.

  ===========================================================================
  2) Scheduled-task persistence (100210 / 100211) — a RULE-SET gap
  ===========================================================================
  Built-in rule 60228 ("A scheduled task was created") fires at level 4 for
  every 4698, so the malicious \Updater task and a benign OneDrive task score
  the same. These read the task's own definition and elevate the ones carrying
  a PowerShell download cradle or an obfuscated / hidden-window launcher.

  The `taskContent` field was confirmed present on a real 60228 alert in this
  lab. Note it arrives HTML-escaped (`&lt;Arguments&gt;...`), so the angle
  brackets of the task XML are encoded while the command text inside is not —
  which is why these patterns key on command tokens and not on XML structure.

  Unlike the LSASS rules, these work on all three hosts: 4698 arrives over the
  Security channel, which every agent is configured to read.

  ===========================================================================
  STATUS AND DEPLOYMENT
  ===========================================================================
  VERIFIED BY LIVE TRIGGER — 24 Oct 2026, THEPUNISHER + wazuh-vm
  (times UTC; the host runs UTC+2)

    wazuh-analysisd -t        clean, no warning          where the original
                                                         raised (7610) and
                                                         discarded its rule
    100200  11:42:39  level 12   powershell.exe OpenProcess(lsass, 0x1FFFFF)
    100201  11:54-11:58  0 alerts from 20 platform EID 10 events
                               (svchost x16, MsMpEng x4). Tested by temporarily
                               adding 0x1000 to 100200's mask so ordinary
                               platform reads had to pass through the chain;
                               mask reverted afterwards.
    100205  11:42:16  0 alerts   MsMpEng.exe read lsass at 0x1010 — the exact
                               mask 92900 matches. Before this rule the same
                               event was a level-12 alert, 32 times out of 32.
    100210  11:48:57  level 12   \ZZ-Verify-Cradle  (IEX + DownloadString)
    100211  11:49:04  level 10   \ZZ-Verify-Hidden  (-nop -w hidden -enc)

    NEGATIVE TESTS — both stayed at built-in 60228, level 4, as required:
    11:49:07  \ZZ-Verify-Benign    defrag.exe C: /O
    11:51:25  \ZZ-Verify-BenignPS  powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass
                                     -File C:\Scripts\DailyReport.ps1

  The second negative test matters more than the first: a scheduled task that
  legitimately runs PowerShell with -ExecutionPolicy Bypass is the realistic
  false positive for 100210/100211, and it did not trip either rule.

  A controlled before/after fell out of the run by accident. The same trigger
  was fired twice from the same host with the same mask, six minutes apart,
  with only the rule's parent changed in between:

      11:36:58  powershell.exe 0x1fffff   <if_sid>92900</if_sid>          0 alerts
      11:42:38  powershell.exe 0x1fffff   <if_group>sysmon_event_10</..>  level 12

  Sysmon recorded all three events; only the second configuration alerted.

  Still not covered: an LSASS read from code injected into an already-excluded
  signed process. See the CallTrace note on 100201.

  Deploy:
      cp local_rules.xml /var/ossec/etc/rules/
      /var/ossec/bin/wazuh-analysisd -t      # must print no WARNING/ERROR
      systemctl restart wazuh-manager

  Coverage limit: Wazuh receives Sysmon from 1 of this lab's 3 Windows agents,
  so 100200 can only fire on THEPUNISHER. See v3/README.md "Known gaps".
-->

<group name="windows,sysmon,credential_access,">

  <!--
    Dump-capable handle opened on LSASS.

      0x1438 / 0x143a            masks MiniDumpWriteDump commonly requests
      0x1f0fff - 0x1fffff        near-full / full access
    0x1f3fff is the mask recorded from notepad.exe during the v3 SharpKatz run
    (Sysmon EID 10, v3 detection screenshot 07) and from powershell.exe in the
    sample above.
  -->
  <rule id="100200" level="12">
    <if_group>sysmon_event_10</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\+lsass\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.grantedAccess" type="pcre2">(?i)^0x(1438|143a|1f0fff|1f1fff|1f2fff|1f3fff|1ffff|1fffff)$</field>
    <description>Dump-capable handle opened on LSASS — possible credential dumping (T1003.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1003.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access,pci_dss_10.6.1,gdpr_IV_35.7.d,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Platform processes observed reading LSASS on this baseline, suppressed.
    Every path is anchored in full, so a payload named svchost.exe running from
    C:\Temp does not match the exclusion and still alerts at level 12.

    Residual limitation, and it is not solved by signature checking: code
    injected into the *real* signed svchost.exe or MsMpEng.exe inherits a
    legitimate SourceImage and is suppressed. Separating that needs the
    CallTrace field — an LSASS read whose stack runs through UNKNOWN frames or
    a non-Microsoft module. CallTrace is present in Sysmon EID 10 and is the
    natural next iteration; it is left out here because this lab has no
    injected-into-svchost sample to test it against, and an untested pattern
    is exactly the failure this file documents.

    100201 and 100205 overlap on purpose. 100201 guards the high-severity path
    and 100205 guards the built-in 92900 directly, so a benign platform read is
    suppressed whichever rule the engine settles on.
  -->
  <rule id="100201" level="0">
    <if_sid>100200</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.sourceImage" type="pcre2">(?i)^C:\\+(Windows\\+System32\\+(svchost|MRT|taskmgr)\.exe|Windows\\+System32\\+wbem\\+WmiPrvSE\.exe|ProgramData\\+Microsoft\\+Windows Defender\\+Platform\\+[^\\]+\\+MsMpEng\.exe|Program Files\\+VMware\\+VMware Tools\\+vmtoolsd\.exe|Program Files \(x86\)\\+ossec-agent\\+wazuh-agent\.exe)$</field>
    <description>LSASS access by a known-benign platform process — suppressed</description>
    <group>credential_access,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Fixes a false positive that exists in the lab today, independent of the
    rules above. Built-in rule 92900 fires at LEVEL 12 for any LSASS read with
    read permissions, including Defender's. Every one of the 32 EID 10 alerts
    Wazuh has ever recorded here is benign — MsMpEng.exe (29), svchost.exe (2),
    MRT.exe (1) — and every one scored level 12. That is a 100% false-positive
    rate on the one credential-access alert the EDR produces, which trains an
    analyst to ignore it.
  -->
  <rule id="100205" level="0">
    <if_sid>92900</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.sourceImage" type="pcre2">(?i)^C:\\+(Windows\\+System32\\+(svchost|MRT|taskmgr)\.exe|Windows\\+System32\\+wbem\\+WmiPrvSE\.exe|ProgramData\\+Microsoft\\+Windows Defender\\+Platform\\+[^\\]+\\+MsMpEng\.exe|Program Files\\+VMware\\+VMware Tools\\+vmtoolsd\.exe|Program Files \(x86\)\\+ossec-agent\\+wazuh-agent\.exe)$</field>
    <description>Routine platform LSASS read — suppresses the level-12 false positive from built-in rule 92900</description>
    <group>credential_access,</group>
  </rule>

</group>

<group name="windows,windows_security,persistence,">

  <rule id="100210" level="12">
    <if_sid>60228</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.taskContent" type="pcre2">(?i)(IEX|Invoke-Expression|DownloadString|DownloadFile|Net\.WebClient|FromBase64String)</field>
    <description>Scheduled task created with a PowerShell download cradle — likely persistence (\Updater pattern)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1053.005</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>persistence,pci_dss_10.6.1,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100211" level="10">
    <if_sid>60228</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.taskContent" type="pcre2">(?i)(-enc\s|-EncodedCommand|-w\s+hidden|-WindowStyle\s+Hidden|-nop\b|-NoProfile)</field>
    <description>Scheduled task created with an obfuscated or hidden-window PowerShell launcher</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1053.005</id>
      <id>T1027</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>persistence,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
